# Slice 333: `fn: g merge(f1)` -- merge copies the Structure the declaration of f1 created in this traversal (an
# ordinary Structure, not a function), and the receiver fn creates from its parts the function g in the namespace of
# this traversal (the author 2026-11-09). g's data is the copy of f1's data at the merge (calls 0) completed by the own
# fields of the code: the counters stay apart (c1 1, c2 2). g's code is the copy of f1's; its free name k is resolved
# from g, created at the root -- k is 5 when g runs (r2 15, r3 25). g is interpreted: no native code exists for it.
int: k 2
fn: f1 (int: a) int (int: calls 0)
    calls := calls + 1
    return: a * 10 + k
end: f1
fn: g merge(f1)
k := 5
int: r1 f1(1)
int: r2 g(1)
int: r3 g(2)
int: c1 f1\data\calls
int: c2 g\data\calls
